From 2e1d9cdbec09fbac35860ae7328b61faac173a5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arena <arena@local>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 01:21:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] peephole(x86): walk the flag flow instead of scanning
 text for consumers

`flag_consumers_are_zf_only` licenses two flag-divergent rewrites: the byte
form of a copy+mask (`movl %ebx,%esi; andl $128,%esi` -> `testb $128,%bl`) and
the memory compare (`movzbl mem,%r; testl %r,%r` -> `cmpb $0,mem`). It walked
the text linearly and skipped two things it should not have.

1. `pushf*` carries `LineKind::Push { reg: REG_NONE }` so that %rsp tracking
   stays correct, and the scan skipped Push/Pop kinds as "stack adjustments
   [that] do not touch EFLAGS" -- before consulting `flags_effect`, which lists
   pushf among the flag readers. An instruction that reads EVERY flag was
   therefore invisible. Reproduced on this commit's parent:

       testb $128, %bl   <- SF = bit 7 of the mask result, not bit 31 (= 0)
       pushfq            <- captures SF as data
       popq %r12
       je .LBB5
       ...
   .LBB5:
       shrb $7, %r12b    <- returns the captured SF

   `flags_dead_after` in the same file consults `flags_effect` first and is
   correct; only this function had the ordering the wrong way round.

2. Flags travel along the TAKEN edge of a conditional jump as well as down the
   fall-through, so stopping at the first flag writer proves nothing:

       andl $128,%esi; je .L1; addl $1,%edi; ...  .L1: js .L2

   The `addl` kills the flags on the fall-through path only; the `je` delivers
   them to the `js` that reads SF. Same mechanism at the `cmpb $0` site in
   dead_writes.rs, whose divergence set is SF for the same reason.

Both are closed by one mechanism: `walk_flag_consumers`, a forward reachability
walk over the flag flow rather than the text. It follows direct branch targets
as well as fall-through, classifies every line through `flags_effect` (so only
real register pushes/pops are skipped), ends each path at the first writer,
call or ret, merges paths through a visited set, and reports `proved = false`
for anything it cannot resolve -- an indirect jump, an external symbol, a label
outside the function -- instead of guessing. An unproved walk licenses nothing.

The guard is also narrowed, from "every consumer tests ZF" to "no consumer
reads SF", because SF is the ONLY flag either rewrite can change: ZF, PF, CF
and OF are identical and both forms leave AF undefined, and for the byte form
SF can differ only when the mask carries bit 7 -- which then needs no walk at
all. That makes the fix strictly more permissive than what it replaces: a
downstream `jc`, `jo`, `jp` or `adc` no longer refuses the fold.

Measured over the whole corpus (818 TUs), this commit's parent vs this commit:
x86-64 identical=816 differing=0 at -O1/-O2/-O3/-Os; `testb $` 22->22 (-O2) and
63->63 (-O1); `cmpb $0` 16->16 and 4->4; compile time paired and interleaved,
min 28.10s -> 27.83s (-0.95%), median +2.38% against a +-9% run spread. An
earlier, coarser version of this fix -- require verified flag-block-locality
after stepping over a conditional edge -- was sound but cost 9 differing TUs at
-O2 and 30 at -O1, including a lost fold in tests/bench/k_memchr.c
(`cmpb $0,(%rdi,%rsi)` becoming `movzbl`+`testl`). It was measured, rejected
and replaced.

Mutation-verified: reverting the pushf/popf kind guard, the edge following, the
`proved` reporting, the SF narrowing or the bit-7 short-circuit each fails its
own pin and only its own (22 tests, exact attribution).
